2068       /* Seeded synthetic repeated binary outcomes, not the abbreviated source data. */
2069       data Resp;
2070       call streaminit(314159);
2071       length treatment sex $1;
2072       array v{4} Visit1-Visit4;
2073       do id=1 to 80;
2074        center=1+mod(id,2); if mod(id,3)=0 then treatment='P'; else treatment='A';
2075        if mod(id,4)<2 then sex='M'; else sex='F';
2076        age=25+mod(id*7,40); baseline=rand('bernoulli',0.4); u=0.6*rand('normal');
2077        do j=1 to 4; v{j}=rand('bernoulli',1/(1+exp(-(-0.5+0.3*(treatment='A')+0.2*baseline+u+0.1*j)))); end;
2078        output;
2079       end;
2080       drop j u;
2081       run;

NOTE: The data set WORK.RESP has 80 observations and 10 variables.

2082       data Resp;
2083       set Resp;
2084       Visit=1;
2085       Outcome=Visit1;
2086       output;
2087       Visit=2;
2088       Outcome=Visit2;
2089       output;
2090       Visit=3;
2091       Outcome=Visit3;
2092       output;
2093       Visit=4;
2094       Outcome=Visit4;
2095       output;
2096       run;

NOTE: There were 80 observations read from the data set WORK.RESP.
NOTE: The data set WORK.RESP has 320 observations and 12 variables.

2097       proc genmod data=resp;
2098       class id treatment(ref="P") center(ref="1") sex(ref="M")
2099       baseline(ref="0");
2100       model outcome(event='1')=treatment center sex age baseline / dist=bin;
2101       repeated subject=id(center) / corr=unstr corrw;
2102       run;

NOTE: PROC GENMOD is modeling the probability that Outcome='1'. One way to change this to model the probability that Outcome='0' is to specify the DESCENDING 
      option in the PROC statement.
